olved. reproduce: the d32 L2 grid run is the lesson model's own recipe, so its checkpoints up to step 2000 must equal the stored training curve (4 decimals).","d16":{"runs":["d16L2","d16L2s8","d16L2s9"],"heldOut":{"n":3,"mean":0.68242,"sd":0.0067868,"min":0.67458,"max":0.68635},"log10Excess":{"n":3,"mean":-1.6348,"sd":0.13761,"min":-1.7937,"max":-1.5551},"copyAccuracy":{"n":3,"mean":0.99407,"sd":0.0052291,"min":0.98814,"max":0.99802}},"d32":{"runs":["d32L2","d32L2s8","d32L2s9"],"heldOut":{"n":3,"mean":0.6793,"sd":0.0052433,"min":0.67348,"max":0.68365},"log10Excess":{"n":3,"mean":-1.692,"sd":0.11772,"min":-1.8245,"max":-1.5995},"copyAccuracy":{"n":3,"mean":0.99341,"sd":0.0060392,"min":0.98814,"max":1}},"reproduce":{"points":11,"pointsEqual":11,"worstAbsDiff":0,"finalAt4000":{"step":4000,"heldOut":0.67348,"excess":0.014979,"copyAccuracy":0.99209,"flops":179306496000,"tokens":1536000}}},"papers":{"about":"What the two papers claim, read from the papers, beside what this toy grid can and cannot say. Kaplan et al. 2020 (arXiv 2001.08361): loss falls as a power law in non-embedding parameters N (embeddings excluded), dataset size D and compute C, with exponents near 0.076, 0.095 and 0.050 over models of about 768 to 1.5 billion non-embedding parameters on WebText2; L(N) is measured on models trained near convergence; for a compute budget they find the best N grows as C to the 0.73, so most extra compute should buy a larger model trained short of convergence. Hoffmann et al. 2022 (arXiv 2203.15556, Chinchilla): over 400 models from 70 million to over 16 billion parameters on 5 to 500 billion tokens, with the learning-rate schedule matched to each run's length, three methods give the best N growing as C to about 0.46 to 0.50 and tokens as C to about 0.50 to 0.54, so parameters and tokens should grow together (about 20 tokens per parameter at their scale); their parametric fit L = E + A / N^0.34 + B / D^0.28 has a constant E for the text's own entropy, the term this lesson knows exactly (the generator floor). They attribute Kaplan's larger model-size exponent mainly to a fixed schedule and small models. This grid has 12 models of 616 to 100544 non-embedding parameters on a 46-word synthetic grammar with a known floor, 4,000 steps at most, one learning rate, a constant schedule, and one seed at most points. Its slopes are descriptions of this toy over this range; they are not estimates of either paper's exponents, and agreement or disagreement with those numbers is not evidence about large language models.","kaplan":{"arxiv":"2001.08361","alphaN":0.076,"alphaD":0.095,"alphaCmin":0.05,"nOptExponent":0.73,"nRange":"about 768 to 1.5e9 non-embedding parameters"},"hoffmann":{"arxiv":"2203.15556","a":[0.5,0.49,0.46],"b":[0.5,0.51,0.54],"E":1.69,"alpha":0.34,"beta":0.28,"models":"over 400, 70e6 to over 16e9 parameters, 5e9 to 500e9 tokens","chinchilla":{"params":70000000000,"tokens":1400000000000},"gopher":{"params":280000000000,"tokens":300000000000}}},"runSeconds":{"d32L3":111.78,"d64L1":123.86,"d32L2":82.525,"d32L2s8":84.938,"d64L2":247.97,"d32L1":43.864,"d32L2s9":80.84,"d16L2":28.81,"d16L3":39.806,"d16L2s8":28.611,"d16L1":16.911,"d16L2s9":29.325,"d8L2":14.439,"d8L1":8.424,"d8L3":19.618,"d64L3":357.31},"seconds":357.43}
